  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface level pressure analysis this afternoon shows an\nanticipated inverted trough firmly over the eastern forecast\narea. Radar trends at the time of writing painted a line of slow\nmoving showers forming over the Charleston metro and climbing up\nthe I-79 corridor. These low topped showers appear to be\nfollowing light southwesterly flow per forecast soundings, with\nthe slow nature prompting close monitoring for potential flash \nflooding. This is especially true for the northern half of the\nforecast area, where 1hr-FFG remains below an inch. While \nrainfall rates seem to be manageable thus far this afternoon,\nwill have to keep in mind the highly sensitive soils throughout\nthe course of the evening and overnight.\n\nModel guidance suggests a slightly northwestern push of\nprecipitation late tonight into Wednesday morning as surface low\npressure begins to churn offshore. Mid to upper level troughing\ncloses off into low pressure, which will continue to provide\nnutritional moisture for additional showers and thunderstorms \nheading into Wednesday. With concern of more rain and already \ncompromised soils, opted to extend the Flood Watch in time \nthrough Wednesday evening to encompass this longer period of \nrainfall and flash flooding monitoring. May also need to\nconsider an expansion in counties if confidence grows for rain\nto park over the Ohio River Valley.\n\nThe center of the upper low dismantles back into an open wave\nThursday morning, with shower coverage slowly diminishing from\nnorth to south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLingering showers over the mountains come to an end on Thursday\nin response to building high pressure at the surface and ridging\naloft. These two features will work in tandem to advertise a\nstretch of dry weather for the end of the work week into the\nweekend. Cooler air filtering in from the northwest will yield\ntemperatures that will resemble Autumn, and a welcome reprieve\nfrom the stretch of heat noted over the past few weeks. Daytime\nhighs will struggle to topple over the 70 degree mark from\nThursday onward, while overnight lows range in the 40s to 50s\neach night through the weekend.\n\nA classic overrunning profile observed on forecast soundings \nfor today and Wednesday will keep the area entrenched beneath \novercast skies for the next few days. As the advancing ridge and\nsurface high build into the area, we should see clearing skies\nbranch out as early as Thursday evening.\n\nNext weather maker on the horizon does not look to approach the\narea until sometime around the middle of next week.",
